{"thoughts":"The bug concerns resolving a non-absolute process argv[0] like \"sh\" via the container's PATH before exec. The likely implementation point is crun's utility code used before exec, especially src/libcrun/utils.c or related headers. I will inspect the utility source around path/exec helper functions to identify whether lookup is using host access or missing PATH search.","command":{"name":"read_range","args":{"project_name":"oci","bug_index":1,"filepath":"src/libcrun/utils.c","startline":1,"endline":260}}}{"thoughts":"The initial utility source only showed generic helpers and no PATH lookup yet; the relevant function is likely further down in utils.c, because the upstream matched fix is described as `utils: fix PATH lookup`. I need to inspect the later section of this same file for a helper that resolves executable paths or builds argv[0] before exec.","command":{"name":"read_range","args":{"project_name":"oci","bug_index":1,"filepath":"src/libcrun/utils.c","startline":260,"endline":620}}}{"thoughts":"The second range still did not reach executable lookup code; it only covered filesystem and read helpers. Since the issue is specifically `$PATH lookup before exec` and the upstream fix was in utils.c, the next focused step is to inspect later lines in utils.c where process/exec helper functions are likely defined, rather than switching files broadly.","command":{"name":"read_range","args":{"project_name":"oci","bug_index":1,"filepath":"src/libcrun/utils.c","startline":620,"endline":980}}}{"thoughts":"The observed helper run_process already uses execvp, so it is not the buggy container payload path. The bug likely lies in the actual container init/exec path, where crun may be passing process.args[0] directly to execv/execve without resolving it against PATH inside the container. The next focused step is to inspect src/libcrun/container.c around the container process launch logic.","command":{"name":"read_range","args":{"project_name":"oci","bug_index":1,"filepath":"src/libcrun/container.c","startline":1,"endline":260}}}{"thoughts":"The first part of container.c only shows setup declarations and the embedded default spec, not the process launch. Since the issue is specifically PATH lookup before exec'ing container process, I need a focused read deeper in container.c where the init process transitions into execve/execv. The immediate next step is to inspect later lines around the likely container entrypoint implementation.","command":{"name":"read_range","args":{"project_name":"oci","bug_index":1,"filepath":"src/libcrun/container.c","startline":260,"endline":620}}}{"thoughts":"The inspected range still only covers sync socket helpers and not the process exec path. The PATH issue likely occurs where the container init finally calls execve with process->args[0], so I need to inspect the next section of container.c for the container entrypoint and exec call before stating the root cause.","command":{"name":"read_range","args":{"project_name":"oci","bug_index":1,"filepath":"src/libcrun/container.c","startline":620,"endline":980}}}{"thoughts":"The relevant exec path is now identified. In container.c the container init calls execvp(def->process->args[0], def->process->args). execvp consults the process environment, but at this point crun may not have installed the OCI process env into environ before exec, so PATH lookup for an unqualified argv[0] like \"sh\" can use the runtime/host environment or fail instead of resolving via the container-configured PATH. The fix should ensure PATH resolution is performed against def->process->env (or set the OCI env before execvp / use execvpe), so unqualified process args are resolved consistently inside the container rootfs.","command":{"name":"express_hypothesis","args":{"hypothesis":"crun-13 is caused by the container process exec path performing $PATH lookup without using the OCI process environment. The final init path calls execvp(def->process->args[0], def->process->args), which searches PATH from the current runtime process environment rather than the configured container process env if that env has not already been applied. For an OCI spec with process.args[0] = \"sh\" and PATH only specified in process.env, this can fail or resolve differently. The repair should make the container exec path search PATH from def->process->env, e.g. by using execvpe with the OCI env array or otherwise setting environ before execvp, so \"sh\" resolves to /bin/sh in the container as reference runtimes do."}}}{"thoughts":"The buggy call site is focused at src/libcrun/container.c lines 649-656. Before changing it, I need a narrow check for existing execvpe usage or portability wrappers in this codebase, so the patch can follow established project style and avoid introducing an unsupported libc dependency pattern.","command":{"name":"search_code_base","args":{"project_name":"oci","bug_index":"1","key_words":"execvpe"}}}{"thoughts":"The search did not reveal an existing execvpe wrapper, and using glibc execvpe directly may still search PATH from the caller environment rather than the supplied envp. I need a focused search for existing environment parsing helpers before adding custom PATH lookup at the container exec site.","command":{"name":"search_code_base","args":{"project_name":"oci","bug_index":"1","key_words":"PATH="}}}{"thoughts":"The focused evidence still points to the init exec site in src/libcrun/container.c: it calls execvp on def->process->args[0], which resolves a bare command such as \"sh\" using the runtime process environment rather than the OCI process env. I need to confirm whether the container environment is applied before this exec, because if not, the simplest safe fix is to use the OCI env array for PATH lookup/execution at this site.","command":{"name":"search_code_base","args":{"project_name":"oci","bug_index":"1","key_words":"environ"}}}{"thoughts":"The container init path at src/libcrun/container.c line 652 uses execvp, so a relative process arg is resolved against crun's own PATH. For OCI process.env, PATH must be taken from the container process environment while executing with that same environment. Since container.c defines _GNU_SOURCE, glibc execvpe is available and directly performs PATH lookup using the supplied envp. I will replace execvp with execvpe and add a comment explaining why the OCI env is used for PATH resolution.","command":{"name":"write_fix","args":{"project_name":"oci","bug_index":"1","changes_dicts":[{"file_name":"src/libcrun/container.c","insertions":[{"line_number":652,"new_lines":["  /* Use the OCI process environment for PATH lookup before exec so relative","     process arguments such as \"sh\" are resolved inside the configured container","     environment rather than against crun's own host environment.  */"]}],"deletions":[],"modifications":[{"line_number":652,"modified_line":"  execvpe (def->process->args[0], def->process->args, def->process->env);"}]}]}}}